{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 36, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the twelfth century. His life story involves complex political maneuvering alongside significant military engagements that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. He belonged to the House of Toulouse and inherited his title after his father Raymond II suffered assassination by the Order of Assassins. This tragic event forced young Raymond to assume leadership during a volatile period filled with external threats from Muslim powers and internal strife among Christian nobility.\n\n*Early Life and Succession Challenges*\nRaymond faced immediate difficulties upon taking power because he was still a minor when his father died. The regency period involved various factions vying for control while the County of Tripoli remained vulnerable to attacks. He eventually secured his position through diplomatic alliances and strategic marriages that strengthened his claim against rival noble families. His ability to navigate these treacherous waters demonstrated his political acumen and set the stage for his future influence in regional affairs.\n\n*Regency and Political Influence*\nThe count later became regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule effectively without assistance. Raymond acted as a stabilizing force during this turbulent era and he often advocated for peace negotiations with Saladin rather than engaging in reckless warfare. This cautious approach earned him both supporters and enemies within the royal court since some factions preferred aggressive military campaigns to expand or defend Christian territories. His tenure as regent highlighted his commitment to preserving the kingdom through diplomacy instead of constant conflict.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Final Days*\nRaymon